{
  "locale": "lg",
  "namespace": "Scheduling",
  "convention": "The same three-state convention as messages/lg.needs-translation.json, for the Scheduling namespace: every key is translated, awaiting a translator, or identical on purpose. EVERY key below is awaiting a translator. The agent that wrote this namespace does not speak Luganda well enough to be trusted with it, and the brief it worked to is explicit that a confident-sounding wrong translation in a health context is worse than an honest English string a reader can recognise as foreign and ask somebody about. src/i18n/i18n.test.ts reads only the BASE catalogues, so nothing enforces this file; it is a worklist for a translator, not a gate. If that test is ever widened to the domain catalogues, this file is already in the shape it expects.",
  "translateFirst": "Scheduling.appointments.hold* and Scheduling.booking.pay* first, then Scheduling.booking.slotTaken*. Those are the strings where misreading the English costs a patient money or a slot: the hold sentences are the only warning that an unpaid booking is released after fifteen minutes, the payment ones are where a patient decides whether to send mobile money now or carry cash to the clinic, and the slot-taken one is the reassurance that a failed booking charged them nothing. The rest of this namespace is navigation and labels, where an untranslated string is an inconvenience rather than a cost.",
  "modesNote": "Scheduling.modes.* carries a product decision that must survive translation: in_person, video, voice and chat are FOUR EQUAL CHOICES. Each note says what the mode is and what it costs in data, and none calls voice or chat a fallback or a lesser option. A translation that renders voice as \"if video does not work\" would undo the thing docs/10-product/prd-h3-teleconsultation.md asks this screen to do.",
